# Supabase
#
# Setting the first two switches admin sign-in to real Supabase Auth. Until
# they are set there is no way into /admin at all, unless ALLOW_DEMO_ADMIN
# below is explicitly turned on.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Admin demo sign-in — LEAVE BLANK.
#
# Set to 1 and any email/password is accepted for /admin. It exists so the CI
# browser suite can drive the admin panel without a database, since the
# panel's data is still localStorage.
#
# The safety property, pinned by a test in src/lib/auth/demo.test.ts: this is
# IGNORED whenever NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
# are both set. A configured deployment cannot be talked into demo mode by
# setting it, which is why it is safe to have at all.
ALLOW_DEMO_ADMIN=

# Signing key for the demo session cookie. Unset means each process invents
# one, so demo sessions do not survive a restart or span instances — the right
# default for something that should never be running in production anyway.
DEMO_ADMIN_SECRET=

# Site
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Analytics + error tracking.
# Left blank pre-launch: lib/analytics.ts buffers events locally and skips
# delivery until a key is present. Setting this is the switch that turns on
# real reporting — see deliver()/deliverError() in that file.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ANALYTICS_KEY=

# Shipping aggregator (Shiprocket).
#
# Leave blank and shipment booking is unavailable; the admin panel keeps the
# manual flow, where an AWB is read off the courier's dashboard and typed in.
# That flow is not going away — it is the fallback when a booking fails.
#
# Both are server-only. SHIPROCKET_PASSWORD is a full account credential, not
# a publishable key, so there is no client-safe half and a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix
# would hand over the shipping account.
#
# SHIPROCKET_PICKUP_LOCATION is the nickname of the pickup address as saved in
# their dashboard; it defaults to "Primary", which is their own default.
#
# NOTE: the adapter is written from Shiprocket's published API and has never
# run against a live account. The first booking needs supervising — see the
# header of src/lib/shipping/shiprocket.ts.
SHIPROCKET_EMAIL=
SHIPROCKET_PASSWORD=
SHIPROCKET_PICKUP_LOCATION=

# Razorpay (India payments — INR only, confirmed scope for Phase 5).
#
# Leave blank and /api/razorpay/order answers 503, which makes checkout fall
# back to the simulated flow — that is the current, intended state.
#
# Only the key id is public. The secret and webhook secret must never gain a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix: that ships them to every browser and hands over the
# ability to forge payments.
#
# The webhook secret is set in the Razorpay dashboard when subscribing an
# endpoint to POST https://<your-domain>/api/razorpay/webhook
NEXT_PUBLIC_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Notification delivery.
#
# Resend carries email; MSG91 carries SMS and WhatsApp. Leave them blank and
# nothing is delivered — messages still queue in the `notifications` table and
# staff can read exactly what a customer would have received, which is the
# honest state for a deployment that cannot send.
#
# Each provider needs BOTH of its variables. A key with no From address or no
# sender id reads as unconfigured on purpose: the provider would reject every
# send, and a half-configured deployment would burn all five attempts on
# something no retry could fix. See src/lib/notifications/transport.ts.
#
# Both keys are account credentials with no client-safe half, unlike the
# Razorpay key id above. A NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix on either would inline it into
# the browser bundle and hand anybody who views source the ability to send
# mail from the shop's own domain, or SMS billed to the shop.
#
# RESEND_FROM must be an address on a domain verified in the Resend dashboard.
# MSG91_SENDER_ID is the six-character DLT-registered header, e.g. GRMNTV.
#
# NOTE: neither adapter has ever run — there are no credentials on any
# deployment and the sandbox they were written in cannot reach either API. The
# HTTP shapes come from the providers' documentation. The first real send needs
# supervising; see the header of src/lib/notifications/providers.ts.
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM=
MSG91_AUTH_KEY=
MSG91_SENDER_ID=

# The shared secret a caller presents to /api/notifications/dispatch, which runs
# one pass over the outbox.
#
# Unset means the route answers 503 and drains nothing — fail-closed, because
# an unset variable is far more likely to be a deployment that forgot than one
# that meant "anyone may drain the outbox". Any long random string will do:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# This one is for callers we control: a pass run by hand against a stuck queue,
# or a scheduler on some other host later. Vercel's own cron uses CRON_SECRET
# below instead. Either variable is enough on its own, and setting both does not
# require them to match.
NOTIFICATIONS_DISPATCH_SECRET=

# The secret Vercel injects into its scheduled requests.
#
# Set this in the Vercel project and Vercel sends `Authorization: Bearer
# <value>` on every cron invocation. The name is Vercel's, not ours — its
# scheduler offers no way to send a different header, which is why the dispatch
# route accepts two variables rather than one.
#
# Only needed on Vercel. Generate it the same way:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the schedule is, and what it is not
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# vercel.json runs the pass once a day, at 02:00 UTC. That is the Hobby plan's
# ceiling: it permits no more than one firing a day, and fires it somewhere
# inside the scheduled hour rather than on the minute.
#
# So the cron is not what delivers an order confirmation — a customer told
# tomorrow morning that yesterday's order went through is worse served than one
# told nothing. Delivery happens inline, from the request that queues the
# message, after its response has gone out. The daily pass is the sweep that
# catches whatever the inline pass could not: a provider that was down, an
# invocation killed mid-send, a message queued with no request behind it.
#
# On Pro the same endpoint takes the every-five-minutes schedule it was
# originally written for, and nothing changes but the `schedule` field.
CRON_SECRET=
